  "attempts": [
    {
      "sql": "SELECT sp.symbol_id AS class_symbol_id, sp.name AS method_name FROM auto_ontology_code.code.class_bases cb JOIN auto_ontology_code.code.class_ancestry ca ON cb.class_symbol_id = ca.class_symbol_id JOIN auto_ontology_code.code.symbol_profile sp ON sp.enclosing_class_symbol_id = ca.class_symbol_id WHERE cb.base_name = 'SQLDatabase' AND cb.base_symbol_id IN (SELECT symbol_id FROM auto_ontology_code.code.symbol_profile WHERE is_class = True) AND sp.is_method = True AND sp.is_public = True AND sp.is_dunder = False AND sp.in_test_file = False GROUP BY sp.symbol_id, sp.name HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT ca.ancestor_symbol_id) = 1 AND sp.symbol_id NOT IN (SELECT base_symbol_id FROM auto_ontology_code.code.class_bases WHERE base_name = 'SQLDatabase')"
    }
  ],
  "usage": {},
  "request": "The production database connectors are the classes that directly subclass SQLDatabase (defined in auto_ontology/connectors/base.py), excluding any class defined in test code (every file under a tests/ folder counts as test code). For each such connector class, which public methods does the class define in its own body that SQLDatabase itself does not define? A method is public when its name does not start with an underscore (so __init__ and other dunder methods are excluded); anything written with def directly in the class body counts as a method, including properties and context-manager methods. Methods inherited without being redefined do not count. Return one row per (connector class symbol ID, method name).\n\nRequested output columns: class_symbol_id (string), method_name (string).\nReturn exactly the requested output columns, using each requested name as the column alias, in the requested order. Return complete repository-relative file paths and complete symbol IDs exactly as stored. When the question asks which files, symbols or modules, return each distinct one once unless it asks for occurrences.",
  "explanation": "This query identifies connector classes that directly subclass SQLDatabase by finding class_bases entries with base_name='SQLDatabase', then uses class_ancestry to trace subclass relationships, and symbol_profile to find public non-dunder methods defined in those connector classes (excluding test file methods). The HAVING clause ensures only directly subclassed classes are included, and the NOT IN filter removes methods that are actually the SQLDatabase class itself.",
  "sql": "SELECT sp.symbol_id AS class_symbol_id, sp.name AS method_name FROM auto_ontology_code.code.class_bases cb JOIN auto_ontology_code.code.class_ancestry ca ON cb.class_symbol_id = ca.class_symbol_id JOIN auto_ontology_code.code.symbol_profile sp ON sp.enclosing_class_symbol_id = ca.class_symbol_id WHERE cb.base_name = 'SQLDatabase' AND cb.base_symbol_id IN (SELECT symbol_id FROM auto_ontology_code.code.symbol_profile WHERE is_class = True) AND sp.is_method = True AND sp.is_public = True AND sp.is_dunder = False AND sp.in_test_file = False GROUP BY sp.symbol_id, sp.name HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT ca.ancestor_symbol_id) = 1 AND sp.symbol_id NOT IN (SELECT base_symbol_id FROM auto_ontology_code.code.class_bases WHERE base_name = 'SQLDatabase')",
